UID:615b2ddf2799b86a@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261002T221157Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Forest Service report highlights effect
 iveness of forestry practices that protect water quality
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nDACF Home\n→\nAbout DACF\n→\nNewsroom\n→ News
 \nMaine Forest Service report highlights effectiveness of forestry practic
 es that protect water quality\nOctober 1\, 2026\nAUGUSTA\, Maine\, October
  1\, 2026 - A new Department of Agriculture\, Conservation and Forestry Ma
 ine Forest Service (MFS) report underscores the effectiveness of environme
 ntally friendly practices during timber harvesting. Released Oct. 1\, the\
 n"Maine Forestry Best Management Practices Use and Effectiveness Data Summ
 ary"\ndraws on hundreds of observations at timber harvesting sites across 
 Maine.\nThis MFS study shows how forestry and timber industry professional
 s use recommended practices statewide. It also demonstrates that the risk 
 to waterbodies greatly increases when they are improperly applied.\nThese 
 measures\, called Best Management Practices (BMP)\, are voluntarily implem
 ented by professionals to help protect water quality in Maine's streams\, 
 rivers\, lakes\, and wetlands. They cover planning and application recomme
 ndations for activities such as stream crossings\, road construction\, and
  controlling erosion and sediment\, the primary pollutants associated with
  timber harvesting.\nMaines forest industry has made great strides in prot
 ecting water resources in recent decades\,\nsaid State Forester and MFS Di
 rector Patty Cormier\n. The improvements weve identified through our BMP m
 onitoring program are a testament to the ongoing value of the training tha
 t loggers\, foresters\, and woodland owners have participated in and appli
 ed. We thank them\, our training partners\, and collaborators for their co
 ntributions to this progress.\nThe report\, released every two years\, is 
 the result of this monitoring from 2024 to 2025. It includes data from 165
  timber harvesting sites and observations on over 700 individual activitie
 s\, including around stream crossings\, contaminants\, and log landing pl\
 nhttps://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13363758
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13363758
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
