UID:794c490e549134c3@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260925T170816Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine CDC Shares Respiratory Virus Season Rem
 inders
DESCRIPTION:Sep 25\, 2026\nAs fall arrives in Maine\, respiratory viruses 
 such as influenza (flu)\, COVID-19\, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)
 \, also return and begin to spread more widely as people spend more time t
 ogether\, indoors. The Maine Department of Health and Human Services’ Ce
 nter for Disease Control and Prevention (Maine CDC) reminds Maine people a
 nd visitors to take steps to keep themselves\, their loved ones\, and neig
 hbors healthy this respiratory virus season.\nRespiratory viruses can caus
 e fever\, cough\, sore throat\, runny or stuffy nose\, muscle aches\, and 
 fatigue. Some people are more likely to get very sick and need medical att
 ention. These viruses can spread when an infected person coughs or sneezes
 . They can also spread from touching the surface with the virus on it and 
 then touching your nose or mouth before washing your hands.\nThe Maine CDC
 \nrecommends\nthe following measures to protect yourself and others:\nStay
  home when you are sick.\nWash your hands frequently with soap and water.\
 nClean and disinfect surfaces and objects that people touch often.\nIf you
  are sick\, consider wearing a mask around others.\nCover coughs and sneez
 es with a tissue or your elbow.\nAvoid close contact with people who are s
 ick.\nGet vaccinated\, as appropriate for flu\, COVID-19\, and RSV.\nVacci
 nation is the most effective way to prevent respiratory viruses. Vaccines 
 can also help make illness less severe and help prevent missed work or sch
 ool. Getting a flu vaccine every year and staying up to date with COVID-19
  and RSV vaccinations\, if eligible\, can help lower the risk of getting s
 ick.\nConsistent with the recommendations of the\nAmerican Academy of Pedi
 atrics\n\,\nAmerican Academy of Family Physicians\n\, and\nAmerican Colleg
 e of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\n\, the Maine CDC recommends the foll
 owing respiratory virus immunization for the 2026-2027 season:\nFlu: Every
 one 6 months and older should receive an annual flu vaccine.\nCOVID-19: Va
 ccination is recommended for\nall children ages 6 through 23 \nhttps://www
 .maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-shares-respiratory-virus-season-reminders-2
 026-09-25
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/news/maine-cdc-shares-respiratory-virus-sea
 son-reminders-2026-09-25
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
